<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deed of Cession (Fiji)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Deed of Cession was the instrument by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau">Ratu Seru Cakobau</a> and other Fijian chiefs ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> on 10 October 1874, making the islands a British colony. Signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levuka">Levuka</a>, it followed the collapse of Cakobau's settler-dominated government under debt. Fiji Day on 10 October marks both the cession and independence in 1970.</p></div>
